# Do not auto-install peer dependencies.
#
# This is npm 6 behaviour, turned back on deliberately, and it is here for one
# concrete reason: Expo.
#
# Nothing in this repository uses Expo. The phone app was ejected from it — see
# `mobile/metro.config.js` and `mobile/babel.config.js`, both of which document
# the move to bare React Native. But `@react-three/fiber`, which the web app
# uses for the transfer scene, declares `expo`, `expo-asset`, `expo-file-system`
# and `expo-gl` as OPTIONAL peer dependencies for its React Native renderer —
# a code path this app never imports, because Vite builds for a browser.
#
# npm 7+ installs optional peers anyway. That pulled in the entire Expo
# toolchain: `expo`, `@expo/cli`, `@expo/metro-config`, `babel-preset-expo`,
# `@expo/vector-icons` and about forty more, none of which is in any bundle and
# all of which showed up in `npm audit`. Measured: 45 Expo packages, and 24 of
# the repository's 24 reported vulnerabilities were inside that subtree.
#
# `--omit=peer` does not help (it changes what is installed, not what is
# resolved) and `overrides` cannot delete a dependency. This is the setting that
# works. With it: 1333 packages become 1147, and the Expo tree is gone entirely.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS. A package that genuinely needs a peer this repository does not
# declare will no longer have it installed silently — it will be missing, and
# something will fail. That is the trade, and it is the right way round: a
# missing peer should be a visible failure and a line in a package.json, not an
# invisible install nobody asked for. The gate is what proves it, and it is
# green with this on.
legacy-peer-deps=true
